BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Humanities in Asia: Going Beyond Make-Do-the Reshaping of a Chines
 e Operatic Tradition in Taiwan — Washington\, DC
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261104T124500
DTEND;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261104T134500
DTSTAMP:20261104T063145Z
UID:4cd3f0e53676b8a6787f011eb4a1@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Arts & Culture\,Washington\, DC\,United States
DESCRIPTION:Kunqu\, a 600-year-old form of Chinese opera known for its poe
 tic lyrics and refined singing\, was among the first Chinese art forms UNE
 SCO recognized as a "Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Hu
 manity" in 2001. The story of how Kunqu survived the political upheavals o
 f the twentieth century runs not only through state-sponsored troupes in C
 hina but also through communities of devoted amateur singers\, known as qu
 you (曲友)\, who carried the tradition to Taiwan. This talk follows thes
 e enthusiasts from their roots in Mainland China to their postwar lives in
  Taiwan\, where they kept Kunqu alive through informal singing gatherings 
 long before it was celebrated as world heritage. When c\nCategory: Arts & 
 Culture\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/humanities-in-as
 ia-going-beyond-make-do-the-reshaping-of-a-chinese-operatic-tradition-in-t
 aiwan
LOCATION:1957 E Street NW\, 1957 E St NW\, Washington DC 20052
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/humanities-in-asia-going-beyond-make-do
 -the-reshaping-of-a-chinese-operatic-tradition-in-taiwan
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:gwu-arts
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:A Gift in Motion — Washington\, DC
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261104T130000
DTEND;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261104T170000
DTSTAMP:20261104T063145Z
UID:84bc87be1f5a1d6c2eff166b9807@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Arts & Culture\,Washington\, DC\,United States
DESCRIPTION:A multidisciplinary showcase from the artists and scholars of 
 the full-time faculty and staff at the Corcoran School of the Arts & Desig
 n. A Gift In Motion loosely draws from Robin Wall Kimmerer’s book The Se
 rviceberry which uplifts Indigenous frameworks of the natural world to adv
 ance the possibilities in collectivity\, reciprocity\, and the mindset of 
 abundance. These are anti-capitalist models and theories that provide insi
 ght into the systems of power we negotiate\, but also offer a lens on how 
 we individually move through our daily lives—together—through historic
 ally fraught landscapes. A keen reminder that working with\, for\, and bec
 ause of each other remains the most human motivation\, eve\nCategory: Arts
  & Culture\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/a-gift-in-mot
 ion
LOCATION:Flagg Building\, 500 17th Street NW\, Washington DC 20006
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/a-gift-in-motion
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:gwu-arts
END:VEVENT
